// Book Six, Chapter 10 (session 66). A Friday in July 1924: Mr. Crane comes to Chicago and to dinner.
// Plays four scenes in a fixed order (the depot, the dinner, the ledger, the lake front) and a short dawn. No night phase: it is one long night.
// The coven fronts it: the lead and the family receive him, sit at the table and read him. Names are always checked with in_circle().
// Reads b6.circle, b6.crane, b6.ames, b6.hall, b6.work, b6.chairs. Sets b6.depot, b6.half, b6.ledger and b6.lake.
// Uses meet(), discover(), chart(), has_gift(), gift_holder(), power(). Ends with rest() and => next.
@chapter b6c10
@title Mr. Crane Comes to Dinner
@book relics-lost-god
@version 1

# start
## The depot
$ b6.depot = "family"
$ b6.half = "asked"
$ b6.ledger = "half"
$ b6.lake = "family"
$ _lead = ""
$ _reader_name = "Dora Valer"
?if coven_size() > 0
  $ _lead = business_lead()
?end
?if _lead != ""
  $ _reader_name = name(_lead)
?end
$ do chart("chicago.depot")
$ do meet("crane")
The Twentieth Century comes in at dusk, a quarter of an hour late, with every blind in the last car drawn. The platform has been cleared. Mr. Ames stands at the end of it in his gray homburg, perfectly still, with a porter on either side who do not know what they are carrying.
:the_platform
* Meet him with the family, in a row, as one meets a guest of the house ?if b6.circle == "all" and coven_size() > 0
  $ b6.depot = "family"
  $ standing += 2
  ?if _lead != ""
    {_reader_name} goes forward first with the lantern, and the others fall in behind, and a very young man in a very good coat steps down out of the dark car, and looks at the row of them, and laughs. It is a short, genuine laugh, with something startled in it. He says that he had not been met by a family since he was twelve.
  ?else
    The family goes forward together, and a very young man in a very good coat steps down, and looks at them, and laughs, short and startled. He says that he had not been met by a family since he was twelve.
  ?end
* Send {_reader_name} to take his bag, and wait at the carriage ?if coven_size() > 0
  $ b6.depot = "lead"
  $ standing += 1
  The young man gives up his bag with a nod, and a look at the one who takes it that is, you think, a little too long. He says that you have a very good steward. It is said lightly, and filed.
* Go alone, and take him yourself
  $ b6.depot = "alone"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  He is shorter than you had thought. He has the face of a boy who has been sixteen for a great many years, and the eyes of someone who has noticed that. He says, "You are the Collins. You look as though you had expected a bigger man."
?if b6.crane == "warm"
  He says that he was pleased by the wire. He says it as one admits to a vice.
?elif b6.crane == "silent"
  He says that he did not hear from you, and that he found it restful, and then that he found it suspicious, in that order.
?else
  He says that your answer was exactly as cool as he had hoped.
?end
---
~> dinner
